00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:57.310
to try and make it the best
place to run SAP in the cloud.

00:22:57.310 --> 00:23:02.190
So let's start with one slide,
my favorite slide of them all.

00:23:02.190 --> 00:23:03.990
This slide basically
is describing to you

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:07.620
the SAP HANA virtual
machines we have available.

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:09.390
And that part of
this conversation

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:12.780
where I see virtual machine
is the most important.

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:15.420
Everybody else in this market
is still largely bare metal.

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:18.030
No one else has a
virtual machine server

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:19.770
above 6 terabytes.

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:23.400
That roadmap says we will
have virtual machines of 12

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:26.520
and 18 terabytes in next year.

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:29.520
We already have, in the light
green box that we'll come back

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:32.970
to, a 7 terabyte VM available
to customers to demonstrate

00:23:32.970 --> 00:23:34.590
that it's possible.

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:36.930
That becomes really important
in this conversation,

00:23:36.930 --> 00:23:43.050
because if you go back in
history, from launch of R3,

00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:46.020
there has been a way that
customers procure and buy

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:48.660
the equipment that
they run SAP on.

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:50.340
Back in the day, this
took a quick sizer

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:52.464
that's still used where
the customers come through,

00:23:52.464 --> 00:23:53.950
and as they want
to roll a project,

00:23:53.950 --> 00:23:57.990
they have to estimate forwards
what their largest hour will

00:23:57.990 --> 00:23:59.470
be in the next three years.

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:01.010
So let's say you're a retailer.

00:24:01.010 --> 00:24:02.180
That's not hard to guess.

00:24:02.180 --> 00:24:09.480
That's probably 8:00 AM Black
Friday in 2022 at this point,

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:10.980
after deployment.

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:14.040
Good luck, however guessing
what that deployment, what

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:16.000
that sizing is.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:17.300
And it ultimately is a guess.

00:24:17.300 --> 00:24:19.410
There is a point where
you can size to a point,

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:21.300
but then you have
to estimate growth,

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:23.220
you have to estimate
change in software.

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:25.650
And so customers tend to buy
a huge amount of equipment,

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:30.360
and hope that three years
from now they're sized to fit.

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:32.915
And if they don't buy enough,
they have a huge problem,

00:24:32.915 --> 00:24:35.040
because they basically have
to trash what they have

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:36.420
and buy again.

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:41.640
If they buy too little, or
actually if they buy too much,

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:43.190
then they've just wasted money.

00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:44.560
And no one wants to waste money.

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:48.230
And so the adult answer to
that is not to do that, right?

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:50.082
Because we really can't
predict the future.

00:24:50.082 --> 00:24:51.790
Three years from now
is a long time away.

00:24:51.790 --> 00:24:54.390
And how are we going to get
to that number predictably?

00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:55.050
We're not.

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:55.680
We never have.

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:57.390
It's always been a guess.

00:24:57.390 --> 00:25:00.241
I think the challenge for me
is as we migrated to the cloud

00:25:00.241 --> 00:25:02.490
and as cloud providers came
online, a lot of them just

00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:06.030
replicated that hardware
model directly in the cloud.

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:07.894
So they came along
and they said you

00:25:07.894 --> 00:25:09.060
have do a three-year sizing.

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:11.518
You have to tell me how big
you're going to be three years.

00:25:11.518 --> 00:25:13.144
You have to sign a
three-year contract.

00:25:13.144 --> 00:25:15.226
That's not what the cloud
is supposed to be about.

00:25:15.226 --> 00:25:17.070
That's not how this
is supposed to work.

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:20.010
And so at Google Cloud we've
approached SAP radically

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:20.760
differently.

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:22.104
We build our own servers.

00:25:22.104 --> 00:25:23.520
We build our own
virtual machines.

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:24.645
We build them from scratch.

00:25:24.645 --> 00:25:26.530
And we do that for a reason.

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:29.670
And the reason is exactly
this, that you fundamentally,

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:31.140
when you come to
us, I don't need

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:32.580
to know how big you're
going to be in three years,

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:33.690
because you don't really
know how big you're

00:25:33.690 --> 00:25:34.830
going to be three years.

00:25:34.830 --> 00:25:36.510
I just need to know
how big you are now.

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:37.884
You can deploy in
that equipment.

00:25:37.884 --> 00:25:40.697
And then as you get
bigger, as you grow,

00:25:40.697 --> 00:25:42.780
as you change over time,
you have the ability just

00:25:42.780 --> 00:25:45.210
to step up through that
rank, because they're all

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:46.980
reconfigurable.

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:50.280
What that does is that allows me
to take a lot of the risk that

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:52.505
exists in your current
environment in sizing

00:25:52.505 --> 00:25:53.880
and provisioning,
a lot of things

00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:56.505
that prevent you going forwards
because you have to try and get

00:25:56.505 --> 00:25:59.077
to a three-year scope that's
relatively fixed and the sizing

00:25:59.077 --> 00:25:59.910
is relatively fixed.

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:02.910
I allow you to take all of
that and put it to the side.

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:06.060
And what you're left with
is an environment where

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:08.850
you can deploy at the size you
are now and grow at the speed

00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:10.080
you want to grow at.

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:12.660
No one has done that before.

00:26:12.660 --> 00:26:15.750
And that unlocks huge
amounts of potential

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:18.750
in your SAP environment as you
grow and you want to change.

00:26:18.750 --> 00:26:21.294
So couple with that a couple
of things on this conversation.

00:26:21.294 --> 00:26:22.710
So first of all
right now, there's

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:26.604
the 4-terabyte HANA
instance, the ultramem-160.

00:26:26.604 --> 00:26:28.020
That's available
to all customers.

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:29.910
Also available in scale out.

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:32.370
That's been available since
June, doing incredibly well.

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:34.740
This box here is
really interesting.

00:26:34.740 --> 00:26:38.970
The 7-terabyte HANA server,
that is the Optane server.

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:41.490
So that's the first Cascade
Lake server in the cloud.

00:26:41.490 --> 00:26:45.300
That's the first Optane
memory server in the cloud.

00:26:45.300 --> 00:26:47.820
It's an amazing box.

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:50.460
SAP published it's own
statistics where that HANA

00:26:50.460 --> 00:26:53.340
server boots 12 times faster
than any HANA server that's

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:54.540
come before it.

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:58.286
Uptime matters in this
world, especially in SAP.

00:26:58.286 --> 00:26:59.910
If your entire
business, if your retail

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:01.910
stores, if your factories,
if your manufacturer,

00:27:01.910 --> 00:27:04.770
buyers, whatever they are, if
they require SAP to be online,

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:06.000
uptime matters.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:07.800
And that server
boots 12 times faster

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:09.540
when you have to patch
it and restart it.

00:27:09.540 --> 00:27:11.434
That's a really big deal.

00:27:11.434 --> 00:27:13.850
In term, we haven't published
any performance tasks for it

00:27:13.850 --> 00:27:14.350
yet.

00:27:14.350 --> 00:27:16.740
But it's the first Cascade
Lake server in the cloud.

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:18.480
We're making huge
investments to get

00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:21.930
to be the highest performing,
the most reliable environment

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:26.250
we can get it to be, in order
to deliver that outcome to you

00:27:26.250 --> 00:27:27.110
as a customer.

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:29.780
Likewise, the 12 and the
18 are in engineering

00:27:29.780 --> 00:27:30.960
to be available next year.

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:33.740
And again, available on
30-second provisioning

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:34.290
globally.

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:37.930
So this is not I tell you I
need an 18-terabyte server

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:40.580
for production, and one for high
availability, and one for QA,

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:41.580
and one for test.

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:44.000
And I pay upfront for them,
and they get delivered to me

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.340
in 12 weeks, and I'm
stuck with those servers

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:48.050
for the next three years.

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:51.090
All of this fleet is available
at 30-second provisioning

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:52.280
globally.

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:54.710
That ability to just click on
a button and start a server

00:27:54.710 --> 00:27:57.740
and run it unlocks a huge
amount of potential in SAP world

00:27:57.740 --> 00:27:59.970
that we've never seen before.

00:27:59.970 --> 00:28:02.040
So that on its own
is interesting.

00:28:02.040 --> 00:28:03.860
But we're not stopping there.

00:28:03.860 --> 00:28:05.960
The other part I'll give
you is a Google tool

00:28:05.960 --> 00:28:07.370
called live migration.

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:11.900
So again, if uptime matters
to you, then what's the best

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:16.530
we can do in keeping SAP
servers up as long as possible?

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:18.180
So one of the
challenges is patching.

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:20.690
So earlier in the
year, obviously, there

00:28:20.690 --> 00:28:22.970
was a little round
of Intel patches

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:24.140
that came out for security.

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:27.080
Every other provider
on the planet,

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:29.000
when that press release
went out on a Tuesday,

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:31.700
informed all of their
customers that they

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:34.250
had 72 hours to take
an unscheduled outage

00:28:34.250 --> 00:28:35.350
and patch the systems.

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:38.807
Now in the world of SAP,
that's not a simple task.

00:28:38.807 --> 00:28:40.640
It's not just shutting
down a single server,

00:28:40.640 --> 00:28:42.306
patching it, and
bringing it back again.

00:28:42.306 --> 00:28:44.685
You have giant clusters
in environments

00:28:44.685 --> 00:28:46.310
of servers that are
all interconnected,

00:28:46.310 --> 00:28:47.510
where they all have
to be shut down

00:28:47.510 --> 00:28:48.960
and they all have to
be patched and they all

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:49.834
have to be restarted.

00:28:49.834 --> 00:28:52.550
And you can lose hours
and hours of work.

00:28:52.550 --> 00:28:54.682
And it's not insignificant
amounts of money.

00:28:54.682 --> 00:28:57.140
We had a customer we talked to
recently here, a $10 billion

00:28:57.140 --> 00:28:58.590
manufacturer.

00:28:58.590 --> 00:29:03.140
And the answer is
the manufacturing

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:07.234
plants can't run if the servers
are not online from SAP.

00:29:07.234 --> 00:29:09.650
So literally, the point where
you get a patch through that

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:11.859
says you have to patch this
within the next 72 hours.

00:29:11.859 --> 00:29:14.066
You're not going to take an
unscheduled outage, which

00:29:14.066 --> 00:29:16.340
is going to result in three
or four hours of downtime

00:29:16.340 --> 00:29:18.659
where the entire
company has to shut down

00:29:18.659 --> 00:29:19.700
while it's being patched.

00:29:19.700 --> 00:29:22.250
In this case $10 billion
a year manufacturer,

00:29:22.250 --> 00:29:23.910
it's a million dollars an hour.

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:26.950
An unscheduled patch is going
to cost them $4 million straight

00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:28.610
away.

00:29:28.610 --> 00:29:30.857
So there has to be a better way.

00:29:30.857 --> 00:29:32.690
There has to be a way
to solve this problem.

00:29:32.690 --> 00:29:34.589
And Google has a way
to solve that problem.

00:29:34.589 --> 00:29:36.130
We have a tool called
live migration.

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:38.050
Again, all of our
servers are VM.

00:29:38.050 --> 00:29:39.970
So what happens
ultimately is the point

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:43.720
where a patch is
released, you don't

00:29:43.720 --> 00:29:45.809
have to shut down your servers.

00:29:45.809 --> 00:29:47.350
You don't have to
do anything at all.

00:29:47.350 --> 00:29:48.880
If you leave a flag
on it in the VM that

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:51.040
says Google is allowed to
patch them, what happens

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:54.160
is we will create a
second parallel server

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:56.800
to the same configuration
of the first.

00:29:56.800 --> 00:29:59.320
We'll pre-patch it with
the patches that required.

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:01.870
We'll sync the server
across while it still runs.

00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:04.000
And we'll transition
you to the patch server

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:07.030
while the systems are
fully operational.

00:30:07.030 --> 00:30:09.000
Zero downtime.

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:12.810
And so again, you go back to
the customer, that's $4 million

00:30:12.810 --> 00:30:15.060
saved every time
there's a patch.

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:17.490
That's a huge amount of money.

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:21.151
We currently run over 3,000
SAP systems at Google Cloud.

00:30:21.151 --> 00:30:23.400
Every single one of them on
the last round of patching

00:30:23.400 --> 00:30:26.430
went through this
process, no issues.

00:30:26.430 --> 00:30:28.119
Just do the math on
the amount of money

00:30:28.119 --> 00:30:30.660
we saved our customers that run,
and the amount of disruption

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:32.010
we took out of the world--

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:34.410
unique to Google Cloud.

00:30:34.410 --> 00:30:37.620
And it's a really,
really big deal.

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:39.330
And it's a really big
deal now, in terms

00:30:39.330 --> 00:30:40.140
of what it can save you.

00:30:40.140 --> 00:30:41.931
It's a much bigger deal
as a representation

00:30:41.931 --> 00:30:43.590
of the engineering
work we're doing

00:30:43.590 --> 00:30:47.700
to not just be another place
to run SAP but to be a better

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:50.510
place to run SAP.

00:30:50.510 --> 00:30:52.660
Couple with that,
let's come back

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:55.940
to the theme of do I really
know what the future looks like?

00:30:55.940 --> 00:30:57.440
Do I really know what I need?

00:30:57.440 --> 00:31:01.300
So again, at this point,
pretty much all everyone else

00:31:01.300 --> 00:31:05.725
in this marketplace is currently
requiring you to contract

00:31:05.725 --> 00:31:07.920
the specific sizes of servers.

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:10.510
So you come in and
say, look, I need--

00:31:10.510 --> 00:31:12.730
I think three years
from now I'm going

00:31:12.730 --> 00:31:14.410
to need a 12-terabyte server.

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:17.080
Then you have to procure right
now a 12-terabyte server.

00:31:17.080 --> 00:31:20.080
So if in the meantime, you
need six 2-terabyte servers

00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:21.730
to get to the
12-terabyte server,

00:31:21.730 --> 00:31:24.002
you've got to contract for
all of that fundamentally.

00:31:24.002 --> 00:31:25.960
There's ways around that,
but generally, you're

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:27.760
going to contract for
more than you need.

00:31:27.760 --> 00:31:30.010
And you're locked into
what you would give.

00:31:30.010 --> 00:31:31.764
What Google Cloud
does is different.

00:31:31.764 --> 00:31:33.430
Because they're all
VM-based, because we

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:35.650
engineer our own
servers, you don't have

00:31:35.650 --> 00:31:37.180
to commit to specific sizes.

00:31:37.180 --> 00:31:38.285
You commit to a capacity.

00:31:38.285 --> 00:31:39.910
And that's what you're
looking at here.

00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:41.860
At some point, I really do
need to get a shot of the GUI.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:43.990
And so the command line-- but
if your command line based,

00:31:43.990 --> 00:31:44.770
you'll like this.

00:31:44.770 --> 00:31:46.936
Basically it's a command
line where what we're doing

00:31:46.936 --> 00:31:53.230
is we're committing to 50,360
gigabytes of HANA memory inside

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:55.120
Europe West 3.

00:31:55.120 --> 00:31:56.830
Once you've committed
to that, we'll

00:31:56.830 --> 00:32:00.850
bill you for it monthly as you
consume it and use it, however,

00:32:00.850 --> 00:32:04.830
you can configure it however
you want it to be configured.

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:09.130
So let's say you need
12-terabytes as an example.

00:32:09.130 --> 00:32:11.110
You can start with 12 ones.

00:32:11.110 --> 00:32:14.350
You can move to six
ones and three twos.

00:32:14.350 --> 00:32:16.240
You can move to three fours.

00:32:16.240 --> 00:32:19.570
You can configure it however you
want to configure that as you

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:22.270
go and as your project changes.

00:32:22.270 --> 00:32:24.020
Never been done before.

00:32:24.020 --> 00:32:29.920
But what it does is it unlocks
you from that false challenge

00:32:29.920 --> 00:32:32.336
you've had in the
past of having to know

00:32:32.336 --> 00:32:34.960
what size you'll be three years,
and you carrying all the risk.

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:37.300
Now you have the
absolute flexibility

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:40.330
to configure and
move your commitments

00:32:40.330 --> 00:32:43.750
as you need them in order to
deliver against a world that's

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:45.040
very hard to predict.

00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:47.757
And again, that's a huge risk
that you take as a SAP customer

00:32:47.757 --> 00:32:49.090
that you no longer have to take.

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:50.680
We will carry that for you.

00:32:50.680 --> 00:32:53.200
We'll help you manage that risk.

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:54.430
Really, really big deal.

00:32:54.430 --> 00:32:56.320
And same, we get a
lot of conversation

00:32:56.320 --> 00:32:58.153
for instance, with
retailers, as an example,

00:32:58.153 --> 00:33:01.169
where they have six, eight
busy weeks in a year.

00:33:01.169 --> 00:33:02.710
And so right now
what they have to do

00:33:02.710 --> 00:33:04.540
is commit to the
hardware they're

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:07.926
going to need for a single
hour three years from now,

00:33:07.926 --> 00:33:09.550
whereas instead they
can commit to what

00:33:09.550 --> 00:33:10.829
they need every day to run.

00:33:10.829 --> 00:33:13.120
And then they can actually
just buy the excess capacity

00:33:13.120 --> 00:33:15.790
that they need for those
six weeks every year.

00:33:15.790 --> 00:33:17.680
It's a vastly more
efficient way to run SAP.

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:19.750
It's a vastly more cost
effective way to run SAP.

00:33:19.750 --> 00:33:22.150
And it unlocks huge potential.

00:33:22.150 --> 00:33:24.070
Again, we talked
about this earlier

00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:25.870
with the SAP Cloud
Platform scenario.

00:33:25.870 --> 00:33:28.360
But if you need a
copy of production,

00:33:28.360 --> 00:33:30.510
you need a copy of QA
in order to run a test,

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:32.260
you don't have to have
a box laying around

00:33:32.260 --> 00:33:33.670
to do that you don't run.

00:33:33.670 --> 00:33:35.720
You just snap up a
copy in 30 seconds,

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:37.970
consume it from your commit,
if you have availability.

00:33:37.970 --> 00:33:40.000
If you don't, you pay
for it by the second.

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:40.630
It's there.

00:33:40.630 --> 00:33:41.200
It works.

00:33:41.200 --> 00:33:41.980
It runs.

00:33:41.980 --> 00:33:44.069
And then when you're done
with it, drop it again.

00:33:44.069 --> 00:33:45.860
Flexibility has never
been achieved before.

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:48.430
And it will change
how SAP is run.

00:33:48.430 --> 00:33:51.327
And however, it's
new to the market.

00:33:51.327 --> 00:33:53.410
And it will take time for
people to understand it.

00:33:53.410 --> 00:33:54.618
But once they understand it--

00:33:54.618 --> 00:33:56.410
I think you see from
METRO, customers

00:33:56.410 --> 00:33:58.810
are starting to
understand that--

00:33:58.810 --> 00:34:00.800
it will make a huge difference.

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:04.520
It will change how SAP is run.

00:34:04.520 --> 00:34:05.940
But that's never enough.

00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:07.490
As I said before,
I was a customer.

00:34:07.490 --> 00:34:10.909
This is not enough just to have
hardware available on demand

00:34:10.909 --> 00:34:12.010
and everything else.

00:34:12.010 --> 00:34:13.489
You got to be able to do more.

00:34:13.489 --> 00:34:17.348
Generally, the
upgrade to S4 is fine.

00:34:17.348 --> 00:34:19.639
But generally you always want
to have a better business

00:34:19.639 --> 00:34:22.159
outcome, not just a
technical upgrade achieved.

00:34:22.159 --> 00:34:23.989
So again, Google
stepped in here in order

00:34:23.989 --> 00:34:26.310
to try and help you get to
a better business outcome.

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:27.642
So just one example of that.

00:34:27.642 --> 00:34:29.600
I'll give you a couple,
but one example of that

00:34:29.600 --> 00:34:32.060
is SAP HANA with BigQuery.

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:35.480
So March 20 of this year,
we announced integration

00:34:35.480 --> 00:34:37.370
with HANA and BigQuery.

00:34:37.370 --> 00:34:38.219
Really big deal.

00:34:38.219 --> 00:34:40.699
So inside HANA--
so for those of you

00:34:40.699 --> 00:34:43.190
who don't know what BigQuery
is, if your SAP customers,

00:34:43.190 --> 00:34:44.523
you're not that aware of Google.

00:34:44.523 --> 00:34:47.449
BigQuery is our data warehousing
in the cloud solution,

00:34:47.449 --> 00:34:49.190
for want of a better answer.

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:52.850
Can handle petabyte levels
of data at insane performance

00:34:52.850 --> 00:34:53.362
levels.

00:34:53.362 --> 00:34:55.820
So you can take huge amounts
of data and put it in BigQuery

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:56.960
and do analysis.

00:34:56.960 --> 00:34:57.970
So that's great.

00:34:57.970 --> 00:35:00.410
However you want to put it
to a practical use case.

00:35:00.410 --> 00:35:03.444
So again, without resorting
always to retail examples,

00:35:03.444 --> 00:35:05.360
right now, we have a
very, very large retailer

00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:07.210
who is a customer
of SAP with HANA

00:35:07.210 --> 00:35:09.860
and a customer of
Google with BigQuery.

00:35:09.860 --> 00:35:14.090
So the SAP data is streaming
in live from the retail stores

00:35:14.090 --> 00:35:15.020
inside HANA.

00:35:15.020 --> 00:35:16.430
All the other data
that they have

00:35:16.430 --> 00:35:18.834
is sitting inside
Google BigQuery.

00:35:18.834 --> 00:35:21.000
What we released is the
smart data access connector,

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:23.270
technically what SAP released,
is a smart data access

00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:26.510
connector for BigQuery.

00:35:26.510 --> 00:35:28.890
When you log into SAP HANA,
you can see the data model

00:35:28.890 --> 00:35:31.790
for SAP HANA and you can see the
data model for Google BigQuery.

00:35:31.790 --> 00:35:34.620
You can join both data
sets together in a query,

00:35:34.620 --> 00:35:37.040
and execute a query
directly in real time

00:35:37.040 --> 00:35:40.350
and get a real-time result back
from both of those analysis

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:41.060
tools.

00:35:41.060 --> 00:35:43.700
So incredibly simple for
the end user to work with.

00:35:43.700 --> 00:35:48.410
But what it does is it unlocks
tremendous opportunities here.

00:35:48.410 --> 00:35:51.240
So one more example
in the retail space.

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:53.420
But we will load cloud
for marketing data

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:55.160
into Google BigQuery for you.

00:35:55.160 --> 00:35:59.120
So I know itemized data
sets that you're currently

00:35:59.120 --> 00:36:02.100
running advertising campaigns,
[INAUDIBLE] Google BigQuery.

00:36:02.100 --> 00:36:04.190
Inside SAP HANA, we
have real-time inventory

00:36:04.190 --> 00:36:05.200
in the stores.

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:07.400
And at the simplest level,
we can join both together

00:36:07.400 --> 00:36:08.900
and come back and
say, hey, you know

00:36:08.900 --> 00:36:11.820
you are advertising
product in this region

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:14.330
that you don't actually have
available at the retail store?

00:36:14.330 --> 00:36:17.540
We think you should move to a
different advertising strategy.

00:36:17.540 --> 00:36:18.566
That's a simple example.

00:36:18.566 --> 00:36:19.940
As a more complicated
example, we

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:21.648
have a retailer recently
who got on stage

00:36:21.648 --> 00:36:24.590
and talked about how we've
done some incredibly cool work

00:36:24.590 --> 00:36:26.810
for them where we're
actually scanning fashion

00:36:26.810 --> 00:36:30.020
images in magazines
and from other sources,

00:36:30.020 --> 00:36:32.660
determining colors and
styles that they can see,

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:35.181
that the image analysis tools
can see in the magazine,

00:36:35.181 --> 00:36:37.680
and then going back to SAP and
joining that data up into SAP

00:36:37.680 --> 00:36:39.888
and saying what do we have
at the retail store that's

00:36:39.888 --> 00:36:42.034
in this color palette,
that's in the style match

00:36:42.034 --> 00:36:43.700
because these are
things that we believe

00:36:43.700 --> 00:36:45.764
people will come in and buy.

00:36:45.764 --> 00:36:47.180
And that's what
we should promote.

00:36:47.180 --> 00:36:49.640
That's what we should
maintain pricing on.

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:52.640
Huge opportunities
here from being

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:56.630
able to join the SAP tooling
with the native cloud tooling

00:36:56.630 --> 00:36:58.160
that Google Cloud
has, and the data

00:36:58.160 --> 00:36:59.780
sets that Google
Cloud has in order

00:36:59.780 --> 00:37:04.190
to get to a far better
result. And again, that's

00:37:04.190 --> 00:37:04.940
our value here.

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:06.760
This isn't just hey,
here's another place

00:37:06.760 --> 00:37:07.760
to run SAP in the cloud.

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:12.530
This is how do we deliver
something much more to you?

00:37:12.530 --> 00:37:15.639
Or how we help you get much
more value from your SAP asset

00:37:15.639 --> 00:37:17.930
and get to the business result
and the business outcome

00:37:17.930 --> 00:37:19.880
you want to get to?

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:23.510
Lastly, one of my
favorite things, robotics.

00:37:23.510 --> 00:37:27.064
So at TechEd Barcelona
and TechEd Las Vegas,

00:37:27.064 --> 00:37:28.730
we had one of these
things on the floor.

00:37:28.730 --> 00:37:30.560
It's a MiR Lifter Shifter.

00:37:30.560 --> 00:37:31.460
So we don't own MiR.

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:32.990
It's a private company.

00:37:32.990 --> 00:37:35.030
It's independent of
us, I should say.

00:37:35.030 --> 00:37:38.000
But what we do is we attempt
to make MiR better and better

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:38.780
for our customers.

00:37:38.780 --> 00:37:40.689
So contained within
the MiR robot,

00:37:40.689 --> 00:37:42.230
there's a communications
package that

00:37:42.230 --> 00:37:44.030
talks back to Google Cloud.

00:37:44.030 --> 00:37:45.830
So whereas right
now you may want

00:37:45.830 --> 00:37:48.870
to automate your
warehousing as example.

00:37:48.870 --> 00:37:52.261
If you may want to automate part
of your distribution network.

00:37:52.261 --> 00:37:53.510
And you want to have robotics.

00:37:53.510 --> 00:37:55.042
That's hard.

00:37:55.042 --> 00:37:56.750
By the time you pick
a robotics provider,

00:37:56.750 --> 00:37:58.857
you buy the networking,
you wire it all up,

00:37:58.857 --> 00:38:00.440
you try and integrate
that back to SAP

00:38:00.440 --> 00:38:04.580
to get maximum efficiency, that
is a huge consulting project.

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:08.216
And a lot of technical work with
networking and everything else.

00:38:08.216 --> 00:38:09.590
With a cloud
package from Google,

00:38:09.590 --> 00:38:11.360
what it will do basically
is MiR will automatically

00:38:11.360 --> 00:38:12.590
connect to Google Cloud.

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:14.173
And what we're working
on is basically

00:38:14.173 --> 00:38:16.850
that inside SAP S/4HANA,
you register the robots

00:38:16.850 --> 00:38:19.190
that you have, they
automatically connect across.

00:38:19.190 --> 00:38:25.790
And then all you do is issue a
goods movement inside S/4HANA

00:38:25.790 --> 00:38:28.220
And the robot will automatically
dispatch itself off,

00:38:28.220 --> 00:38:30.659
grab the shelving or grab
the package that you want,

00:38:30.659 --> 00:38:33.200
and bring it back to the person
that you need at your packing

00:38:33.200 --> 00:38:35.090
station to do the packing.

00:38:35.090 --> 00:38:38.120
That's far beyond
again, just running

00:38:38.120 --> 00:38:40.250
SAP on someone else's servers.

00:38:40.250 --> 00:38:42.830
This is really working
towards the future.

00:38:42.830 --> 00:38:44.690
We all understand that
robotics are coming.

00:38:44.690 --> 00:38:46.850
To be able to do it
that easily and to be

00:38:46.850 --> 00:38:49.266
able to improve your business
at that level of efficiency,

00:38:49.266 --> 00:38:51.290
is something that makes
us radically different.

00:38:51.290 --> 00:38:52.706
And again, that's
part of the work

00:38:52.706 --> 00:38:54.680
we're doing with SAP
to deliver something

00:38:54.680 --> 00:38:57.740
far beyond just the same
SAP running somewhere else.

00:38:57.740 --> 00:38:59.810
So that bit's cool, right?

00:38:59.810 --> 00:39:02.960
Being able to run SAP or
integrate your robotic package

00:39:02.960 --> 00:39:05.560
back to SAP and have it go
grab things for you is cool.

00:39:05.560 --> 00:39:06.925
Let's go one more, however.

00:39:06.925 --> 00:39:07.550
This is Google.

00:39:07.550 --> 00:39:08.800
We don't do think small.

00:39:08.800 --> 00:39:11.740
So embedded in the
robot is a lens package.

00:39:11.740 --> 00:39:14.170
As it drives around your
distribution center,

00:39:14.170 --> 00:39:15.250
it can read the shelves.

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:17.902
It can see what's on them.

00:39:17.902 --> 00:39:19.110
It can identify what's there.

00:39:19.110 --> 00:39:21.235
What you're seeing as the
graphic comes around here

00:39:21.235 --> 00:39:24.229
is it will identify boxes
that are sitting on the shelf.

00:39:24.229 --> 00:39:26.020
And then it will identify
individual boxes.

00:39:26.020 --> 00:39:28.060
And it can read barcodes
and it can read text.

00:39:28.060 --> 00:39:29.893
And it can tell you how
much inventory there

00:39:29.893 --> 00:39:31.240
is in any given location.

00:39:31.240 --> 00:39:34.180
And it can communicate
that back to SAP.

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:36.190
So just picture that world.

00:39:36.190 --> 00:39:39.790
You have a set of robots you
drop in your distribution

00:39:39.790 --> 00:39:40.290
center.

00:39:40.290 --> 00:39:41.873
They start to drive
themselves around.

00:39:41.873 --> 00:39:43.880
They map out the distribution
center themselves.

00:39:43.880 --> 00:39:45.880
They identify everything
that's inside that distribution

00:39:45.880 --> 00:39:46.510
center.

00:39:46.510 --> 00:39:48.310
They communicate it back to SAP.

00:39:48.310 --> 00:39:50.200
And from there forward,
you have constantly

00:39:50.200 --> 00:39:53.234
running live updated
inventory that's sitting

00:39:53.234 --> 00:39:54.400
in your distribution center.

00:39:54.400 --> 00:39:56.080
And that's just
one small use case.

00:39:56.080 --> 00:39:58.997
But it's an incredible
jump forwards and just

00:39:58.997 --> 00:40:00.580
a representation of
the sort of things

00:40:00.580 --> 00:40:03.520
we're working on with SAP to
give you something that's just

00:40:03.520 --> 00:40:06.130
vastly more than anyone's
attempted to do before

00:40:06.130 --> 00:40:07.990
or achieved at this level.

00:40:07.990 --> 00:40:10.120
So thanks for your time.

00:40:10.120 --> 00:40:11.287
That's a very short summary.

00:40:11.287 --> 00:40:13.828
If you weren't aware, I can go
on for a long time about this.

00:40:13.828 --> 00:40:16.000
And we'll happily come
visit you and go on

00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:18.541
for a long time about all the
things we're doing differently.

00:40:18.541 --> 00:40:21.426
That's just a really small
snapshot of what we're doing.

00:40:21.426 --> 00:40:23.800
Stay tuned for the live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:40:23.800 --> 00:40:26.136
minute to answer some of the
questions you've submitted.

00:40:35.670 --> 00:40:36.430
Welcome back.

00:40:36.430 --> 00:40:38.412
We're still taking
questions in the Q&amp;A chat.

00:40:38.412 --> 00:40:39.870
But we decided to
take a couple out

00:40:39.870 --> 00:40:41.250
here just to answer them live.

00:40:41.250 --> 00:40:42.180
Isabell rejoins me.

00:40:42.180 --> 00:40:43.560
Thank you one more time.

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:45.720
So quickly, question number one.

00:40:45.720 --> 00:40:49.550
How do you bridge from the work
the team is doing with ABAP, as

00:40:49.550 --> 00:40:51.085
an example, into the cloud?

00:40:51.085 --> 00:40:52.260
ISABELL PETZELT: Right.

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:55.230
That is exactly a
fundamental step

00:40:55.230 --> 00:40:58.380
of the journey for our customers
from on premise applications

00:40:58.380 --> 00:41:00.690
into the cloud because
they have huge investments

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:03.930
in their on-prem systems that
they've built over decades.

00:41:03.930 --> 00:41:06.540
And now they want to have
a move into the cloud

00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:07.840
at their own pace.

00:41:07.840 --> 00:41:10.920
So that is why they can utilize
SAP Cloud Platform with all

00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:14.640
of the latest technologies,
but infusing those technologies

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:16.320
into the core.

00:41:16.320 --> 00:41:18.640
And for example, ABAP
developers, which

00:41:18.640 --> 00:41:19.765
are a very special species.

00:41:19.765 --> 00:41:22.200
And I know a lot of customers
found that very funny

00:41:22.200 --> 00:41:24.150
when we announced
that ABAP is now

00:41:24.150 --> 00:41:26.610
also available in the cloud.

00:41:26.610 --> 00:41:30.540
They can transfer that skill
set to develop on SAP Cloud

00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:34.140
Platform, utilizing
those technologies,

00:41:34.140 --> 00:41:37.650
and help those developers
really on their journey

00:41:37.650 --> 00:41:39.995
into the cloud, securing
the investments,

00:41:39.995 --> 00:41:41.370
while at the same
time of course,

00:41:41.370 --> 00:41:43.036
they have the choice
to develop on Cloud

00:41:43.036 --> 00:41:45.160
Platform in any language.

00:41:45.160 --> 00:41:48.020
So it's a whole new
world to recruit also

00:41:48.020 --> 00:41:50.159
and leverage different
development skill sets

00:41:50.159 --> 00:41:50.700
in the cloud.

00:41:50.700 --> 00:41:52.491
PAUL YOUNG: I'm choking
back laughter here,

00:41:52.491 --> 00:41:54.714
because I started out
as an ABAP developer.

00:41:54.714 --> 00:41:56.130
So after the cameras
are off, I'll

00:41:56.130 --> 00:41:57.990
ask you what species
exactly you think I am,

00:41:57.990 --> 00:41:59.405
because apparently I
am a special species.

00:41:59.405 --> 00:41:59.660
ISABELL PETZELT: Oops.

00:41:59.660 --> 00:42:01.510
PAUL YOUNG: But there you go.

00:42:01.510 --> 00:42:05.712
Yeah, me and all the other
ABAP developers out there.

00:42:05.712 --> 00:42:07.170
The second question
that came in is

00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:08.280
we're starting
with dev and test.

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:09.670
Production is a
bigger challenge.

00:42:09.670 --> 00:42:11.310
What are you doing to make
production deployments easier?

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:12.539
I guess that one's mine.

00:42:12.539 --> 00:42:13.705
ISABELL PETZELT: I think so.

00:42:13.705 --> 00:42:14.800
PAUL YOUNG: Good question.

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:18.000
I mean, a lot in this
space we see customers

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:20.430
who it's relatively easy
to pull your credit card

00:42:20.430 --> 00:42:22.440
and buy a little dev
box and play with it.

00:42:22.440 --> 00:42:24.441
And then move tests across
[INAUDIBLE] scenario.

00:42:24.441 --> 00:42:26.315
Production clearly is
a much bigger challenge

00:42:26.315 --> 00:42:28.380
around security and
networking and performance,

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:31.200
and all the stuff
that comes with it.

00:42:31.200 --> 00:42:32.910
Key focus for us.

00:42:32.910 --> 00:42:35.595
I think if all any of
us get is dev and test,

00:42:35.595 --> 00:42:37.470
and customers don't
actually move production,

00:42:37.470 --> 00:42:39.477
we don't make the final move.

00:42:39.477 --> 00:42:42.060
A couple of key things I would
highlight for you, one of which

00:42:42.060 --> 00:42:44.250
is automatic configuration.

00:42:44.250 --> 00:42:46.740
Tremendous number of customers
we talk to right now,

00:42:46.740 --> 00:42:49.020
who frankly have a
production server,

00:42:49.020 --> 00:42:52.190
don't have HA, don't have
DR, and run a huge risk.

00:42:52.190 --> 00:42:56.020
In our world, that's the sort of
stuff that's hard to configure.

00:42:56.020 --> 00:42:58.050
As a DBA network
engineer, you make

00:42:58.050 --> 00:43:00.720
you figure that three, four
times in your lifetime.

00:43:00.720 --> 00:43:04.946
It's really easy to do wrong
and then have huge problems.

00:43:04.946 --> 00:43:06.570
So one of those
examples really quickly

00:43:06.570 --> 00:43:09.079
is we have completely automated
provisioning for the VM.

00:43:09.079 --> 00:43:11.620
So when you provision your SAP
system in, you don't just say,

00:43:11.620 --> 00:43:14.841
hey, I want an SAP system and
I want an HA and I want a DR.

00:43:14.841 --> 00:43:16.590
And I wonder why I
pull the network ports.

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:20.610
I'm going to manage all of the
configuration on the replicas.

00:43:20.610 --> 00:43:22.080
Completely automate provision.

00:43:22.080 --> 00:43:24.304
Pick a primary site,
an HA site, a DR site.

00:43:24.304 --> 00:43:25.720
Completely automated
provisioning,

00:43:25.720 --> 00:43:29.797
which allows us to get
to, with confidence,

00:43:29.797 --> 00:43:32.130
get to roll out of production
workloads in Google Cloud.

00:43:32.130 --> 00:43:36.810
The other one I'll add quickly
as we announced at TechEd Vegas

00:43:36.810 --> 00:43:39.840
is we're the first provider
to have back end integration.

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:41.730
Incredibly cool if you're
backing up your SAP

00:43:41.730 --> 00:43:43.309
environments for
production purposes,

00:43:43.309 --> 00:43:45.600
instead of what currently
happens where you take a disk

00:43:45.600 --> 00:43:48.030
snapshot, and you have to
copy it to some other storage.

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:49.740
And you're maybe trying to
replicate it at other places

00:43:49.740 --> 00:43:52.392
around the world where you're
responsible for copying files

00:43:52.392 --> 00:43:53.850
and hoping they
don't get corrupted

00:43:53.850 --> 00:43:55.290
and everything along the way.

00:43:55.290 --> 00:43:57.870
Basically back end
backs up a blob storage

00:43:57.870 --> 00:43:59.830
of your HANA instance.

00:43:59.830 --> 00:44:01.680
You can replicate
it multiple places

00:44:01.680 --> 00:44:03.870
around the world automatically
using Google Cloud.

00:44:03.870 --> 00:44:05.400
And then we can
recover that back

00:44:05.400 --> 00:44:07.650
into a fully functioning
copy of your system

00:44:07.650 --> 00:44:11.040
in the event of an outage or
something happening typically

00:44:11.040 --> 00:44:12.540
in under an hour.

00:44:12.540 --> 00:44:14.869
That sort of stuff
allows us to try again--

00:44:14.869 --> 00:44:17.410
the comparison isn't just can
I run what I have in the cloud?

00:44:17.410 --> 00:44:18.180
The comparison is, wait.

00:44:18.180 --> 00:44:20.200
If I have fully automated
provisioning, I have HA,

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:21.824
I have DR, I have
back end integration.

00:44:21.824 --> 00:44:25.290
I have all of that stuff, I have
a provably better environment.

00:44:25.290 --> 00:44:27.659
And so we invest at a
lot of different levels.

00:44:27.659 --> 00:44:29.700
We've talked about this
already, from all the way

00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:32.715
up in the AI and ML to down
at the street operation.

00:44:32.715 --> 00:44:35.910
All of it has that purpose
of making this the best

00:44:35.910 --> 00:44:37.440
place you could
possibly run SAP,

00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:40.232
and dealing with real problems
that real customers have.

